# Two files, and everything else under system/ is meant to be COMMITTED —
# see DESIGN §4.4. Once a concern's truth is a JSONL that git can merge, there is
# nothing derived left to hide, and hiding it would defeat the point of choosing files.
#
# The database is what survives: it is a rebuildable index of those files now, and an
# unmergeable binary either way. The logs are this machine talking to itself and would
# conflict on every line.
system/jaira.db
system/jaira.db-wal
system/jaira.db-shm
system/logs/

# The key that signs this machine's approvals (SPEC §7.5.5). Committing it would hand
# every clone the ability to mint approvals for this disk.
system/machine.key

# Credentials. .env.local is machine-local by convention and is the first project link of the
# secret chain (DESIGN §8.1). Its sibling .env is deliberately NOT ignored: a project that wants
# to commit a non-secret default has to be able to.
.env.local

# This workspace's id in the shared database (decision 0013 §4). Committed, every clone of the
# repository would claim the same tasks — which this repository did until 2026-10-06.
system/workspace.id

# The project's setup for this machine alone (decision 0027).
setup.local.json
setup.local.yaml
setup.local.yml

# This project's settings on this machine (Settings › this project › this machine): a workspace
# is one folder on one machine, so a clone elsewhere must not take them.
settings.local.json

# This project's files on this machine — its permission sets under local/ (Settings › this project ›
# this machine, 2026-11-18): a clone elsewhere must not take them.
local/
